when gary johnson ten days ago tabbed as  his potential running mate former  massachusetts governor william weld it  led to one of the largest bursts of  attention in the history of the modern  libertarian party people all throughout  the media were writing there's some  strange new possibilities of foot for  the third party what they probably  didn't count on was that at the  libertarian party convention itself  which is ongoing in orlando florida that  pick would be met with a lot more  skepticism so we're here today with  former governor william weld to talk  about it thank you for joining us a  pleasure thank you so to what do you  attribute this skepticism with which you  have been greeted by some people here  well the members of the party don't know  me i mean they haven't seen me i've been  out of politics ten years they don't  know that on my first day in office in  massachusetts I started my first press  conference by saying fellow libertarians  they don't know that in law school my  favorite author was Friedrich Hayek the  author of the constitution of Liberty  and the road to serfdom they don't know  that when I was governor I went to ed  cranes Cato Institute parties the annual  one in Arizona  virtually every year and I've been a  from time to time financial supporter of  Cato  they're my kind of people I when I went  as a fairly new governor and gave a  speech at the convention in Houston that  nominated George W against Pat Buchanan  all the new governors got to make a  short speech and my began I want the  government out of your pocketbook and  out of your bedroom I'm an economic  conservative and I'm socially tolerant  and inclusive that's some of my  political positions let's talk about  that aspect all right you've been in  politics or around politics for quite a  long time describe kind of the both the  ideological opportunity and then the  pragmatic opportunity when faced with  two candidates like this and two  unfavorability kind of ratings like this  well I think and again nothing's nothing  certain and  not assuming dairy and I'll be the  nominees but just for the sake of  argument if we were I think we have an  opportunity to run right up the middle  because forty to fifty five percent of  the electorate thinks of themselves as  economically conservative and socially  tolerant and that's neither party the  Republicans are not socially tolerant  they want to tell people whether or not  they can have an abortion and tell gays  and lesbians whether they can get  married and live happily ever after and  and so on they want to round up the  immigrants this is a nation of  immigrants I'm sorry you know anyone who  wants to round up the immigrants and  build a huge wall has not read a history  book in a long time the most famous wall  in the 20th century was the Berlin wall  which was completely infamous but we're  gonna have to have the rent of Ronald  Reagan of Mexico shaking his fist on the  other side of the wall saying mr. Trump  you know tear down this wall and he's  gonna have to tear it down cuz we will  be on the wrong side of history in 2006  you almost ran for governor of New York  as a libertarian you eventually withdrew  nomination that some people are upset  about that what has been your  relationship with the Libertarian Party  if at all in the ensuing 10 years I've  admired Gary's embrace of the party in  his run in 2012 I mean I was a finance  co-chair for Mitt Romney in New York in  that race and in 2008 so I couldn't  really cross party lines so speak and  endorse Gary but I told my friends  that's that's a guy you can go vote for  and feel proud about Gary and I were  governors together and late 90s his  first term was my second term so we had  overlap there no I guess since I've had  a crush on the libertarian party from  afar I did have the designation in New  York and I went to them and said please  please please I saw Audrey this  afternoon who actually nominated me from  the floor for the  Libertarian Party in 2006 well that was  a semi disastrous race I mean I crashed  and burned as being a carpetbagger even  though I'm actually a native of New York  State but nobody would believe that  because I've been governor of  Massachusetts last night they had the  vice presidential debate I've heard a  lot from a lot of people on the floor  who were very upset that you said that  the United Nations the United States  should belong to the United Nations what  does it make you feel like when the  party that you want to represent  includes a lot of people who think  that's crazy  well I didn't see that in the platform  which I read in its entirety this  afternoon and I was glad I didn't see it  because I found the world a lot for  client business and I see a lot of  suffering in Africa and Asia and I do  think there are things that the United  Nations can be useful for you know I'm  on board with those who think that NATO  is suffering from a case of mission  creep and that's particularly bad for a  country like ours that has intervention  creep in its recent past and I agree  with Gary and that libertarian party we  got to get away from that no more boots  on the ground no more blood and yet you  endorsed John Kasich he's a huge  intervention you hear him talking about  foreign policy he wants to you know  topple the dictatorship in North Korea  and Syria and redline here and punch  Russia in the nose there what were you  thinking about endorsing John Kasich my  relationship with Kasich is on budgetary  issues Newt Gingrich asked me to come to  Congress in 1995 right after Newt and  those folks had gotten in to to persuade  his members they could take tough votes  on economic issues and budget issues and  still get reelected and I went down  there with John Engler from Michigan  tommy Thompson from Wisconsin we did  that successfully and and Kasich was the  Boy Wonder chair of the House Budget  Committee that Newt had leapfrogged over  12 other people that become chairman  because he did his own budget every year  and he was the acknowledged master of  the budget in the house and he was a  long cool drink of water because he knew  exactly what had to be done in terms of  balancing the budget as we have done in  a state level Senate was a much longer  longer row 200 chairman said do we  really have to balance the budget in  seven years couldn't it be nine years  well that's that's not with the program  as they say so I got to know Kasich very  well there in an area where he's an  authentic American Hero namely the  federal budget you started in politics  your governorship is sort of associated  with the 1990s there was such a thing as  the kind of socially liberal Republican  governor or politician that wasn't that  was a category that existed doesn't seem  to exist I associate it was sort of a  good government mitch Daniels type of  thing people who are interested in in  issues like privatization and other  things where did that go began to go in  1994 when things became much more  partisan when I used to see Newt when he  was Minority Leader before he was  speaker he had a saloon door on his  office because the Dems didn't want him  to be able to have a private meeting  when Newt became Speaker he didn't  forget that so you know the atmosphere  became a little bit more pained in both  directions and then you had  redistricting in 2000 1992 and 2002 and  2012 that just drove both parties to the  extreme because they want to protect  their own members so they gerrymander  the congressional seats so that no  incumbent is going to be defeated and  that means that the only thing an  incumbent is scared of is a challenge  from the right in the Republican Party  or from the left in the Democratic Party  so you get much more extreme members  many fewer who are interested in  compromising in the middle the effect  has not been good for the United States  what has to happen in order for the  Libertarian Party ticket to achieve what  you imagined to be its highest possible  well I don't think we're that far away  in this of all years McGarry is polling  Gary Johnson at 10 percent all by  himself without any bursts of recent  publicity if he and I got a got  to be nominees and got a burst of  publicity that would be a 15% maybe and  that's the level where you get into  presidential debates and you know I  could go around to my fundraising pals  from when I was Pete Wilson's national  finance chair when I was Mitt Romney's  finance co-chair in New York and see if  we could attract many angels of  libertarian leanings and some of these  guys you wouldn't have to persuade about  the Libertarian Party they lean that way  and they could potentially help to fund  either a super PAC or a 501 C 4 which is  another type of account where people can  make large contributions at the same  time I think we should have a Bernie  Sanders web based fundraising operation  because we have an appeal to Millennials  so you need a combination of media  attention and and money in the kitty and  then I think people really have to take  us seriously and we both know what we  think and we can string two or three  sentences together so then we get into  the national dialogue and I think that  20% goes to 25% and from that point it's  Katie bar the door because if there are  three parties there with two months ago  or a month and a half ago or even even a  month to go and it's 45 35 25 that's too  close for the pundits to write anybody  off in a crazy year like this thank you  very much is for reason TV I am Matt  Welch
 

